! izba VNC desktop -- X resource defaults for the bundled xterm.
!
! Loaded through XENVIRONMENT (izba-init's `vnc_env`), which every Xt
! application reads at startup. That is what makes these apply to `xterm`
! however it is launched -- the openbox root menu, the lxpanel Applications
! menu / Run dialog, pcmanfm's "Open Terminal", or typed by hand in an
! already-open terminal -- with no wrapper script and no per-launcher argv.
! Explicit command-line flags still win over everything here.
!
! WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: the bundle deliberately ships no
! /usr/share/X11/app-defaults/XTerm, and the file inside the container
! belongs to the user's IMAGE, which need not ship X11 data at all. So the
! terminal ran on xterm's COMPILED-IN defaults, which are 1980s defaults:
! latin-1 decoding, the `fixed` bitmap font, latin-1 window titles. Any TUI
! that draws in UTF-8 -- Claude Code, but equally htop, vim, or git's box
! art -- came out as strings of `â` (user-reported).

! --- encoding: izba's terminal is UTF-8, unconditionally ---------------
! `locale: false` + `utf8: 1` forces UTF-8 decoding regardless of the
! image's locale environment. Two reasons it is forced rather than derived:
!   * `-u8` ALONE IS IGNORED. With the default `locale: medium` the locale
!     decides the encoding and the utf8 resource is overridden -- verified:
!     `xterm -u8` with no UTF-8 locale still renders mojibake.
!   * The alternative, trusting $LANG, is image-dependent. The desktop
!     preamble does default LANG to C.UTF-8 (so programs INSIDE the shell
!     emit UTF-8 too), but an image that pins a non-UTF-8 locale, or a libc
!     without C.UTF-8, would silently fall back to the broken rendering.
! `locale: false` also keeps xterm from trying to exec `luit` for a
! non-UTF-8 locale -- the bundle ships no luit.
XTerm*locale: false
XTerm*utf8: 1
! OSC 0/2 titles are UTF-8 too. Without this, xterm mangles a title
! containing any non-latin-1 byte down to a single junk character, which is
! why the window decoration read as garbage for a Claude Code session
! (its title carries U+273B).
XTerm*utf8Title: true

! --- font: scalable, wide coverage, DPI-independent --------------------
! DejaVu Sans Mono (bundled) covers box drawing, block elements, braille
! and the dingbats a TUI draws with; xterm falls back per glyph through
! fontconfig to the bundled Symbola for the rest (arrows, Miscellaneous
! Technical such as U+23BF/U+23F5, and monochrome emoji), so a missing
! glyph is a blank box only for scripts NEITHER font covers (CJK).
! The size is given in PIXELS, not points: point sizes depend on the X
! server's DPI, and the desktop is resized by the browser at will.
XTerm*renderFont: true
XTerm*faceName: DejaVu Sans Mono:pixelsize=15
XTerm*geometry: 100x30
XTerm*saveLines: 10000

! --- keyboard/clipboard behavior a bare xterm gets wrong ---------------
! Backspace must send DEL and the tty's erase char must agree with it; the
! compiled-in default (BS + whatever the pty inherited) is what makes
! backspace print ^H instead of erasing in a shell or a TUI. This is the
! same pairing Debian's app-defaults apply -- which the guest does not have.
XTerm*ttyModes: erase ^?
XTerm*backarrowKey: false
! Alt as a prefix (readline word motions, TUI alt-bindings) rather than
! eight-bit input.
XTerm*metaSendsEscape: true
! The session is reached through a BROWSER: KasmVNC syncs the CLIPBOARD
! selection with the browser, not PRIMARY, so a mouse selection that only
! landed in PRIMARY could not be pasted out of the desktop at all.
XTerm*selectToClipboard: true
